# Two separate jobs, both of which need this file to exist.
#
# 1. export-subst on _build_info.py.
#
# `git archive` emits tracked files only, which is what makes it a safe way to
# build a clean artifact -- but it also strips the .git directory, so a build
# from an archive has no way to ask git what commit it came from. The
# export-subst attribute is git's own answer: it substitutes $Format: tokens
# into the file's content at archive time, so the commit travels inside the
# tree rather than beside it.
#
# Measured before this line was written, three arms plus a discriminator:
# with this attribute, `git archive` rewrites the literal to the real 40-char
# sha; WITHOUT it, under the identical archive command, the literal survives
# untouched. The control is what makes the first arm mean anything -- it rules
# out `git archive` substituting these tokens unconditionally.
_build_info.py export-subst

# 2. text eol=lf for anything a shell executes.
#
# This box has core.autocrlf true and the repository shipped no .gitattributes
# at all, so a raw clone on Windows materialised every shell script with CRLF
# line endings. A CRLF script is not a cosmetic problem: /bin/sh reads the \r
# as part of the interpreter path or the argument and fails in ways that do not
# name the cause. It also changes the file's hash, so two checkouts of the same
# commit disagree -- avocet had to force LF by hand in its worktrees to make a
# mutation script hash 4b95b510af4ebef1e30681937c241f84 and match this lane's
# copy. That hand-forcing is the workaround this line removes.
# Measured by avocet, 2026-09-14; closed here because this file had to exist
# anyway.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Python and the packaging metadata are executed or parsed by tools that are
# newline-agnostic, but pinning them keeps a diff from turning into a whole-file
# rewrite on a Windows clone.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
